Carafe: with SlimReturnPoints, the code after a call (a return point of the multiblock) no longer stores its block's
header into the thread's state; the multiblock's main entry still does, and so does the interrupt check at every
entry. A fault, a suspend or an unaligned atomic in that code first syncs the header: when the host PC is not inside
the block the state names, FEX walks back from the PC to the nearest adr/str pair that stores a header, and takes that
block once the PC lies within its size. The WoW64 exception handler syncs before anything reads the header, and so
does RestoreRIPFromHostPC. A block that needs far jumps keeps the stores. W7-M3 on the console: the two entry stores
cost 2 cycles an entry. Off by default.
MIT, as the files it changes.

